For Q1/Q2, these are the comparable constructs and the choices they support:

1. **INTERRUPT stub �?" direct precedent for 2; functioning counterparts support 7.**

   [cpu_sh.c:527](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_sh.c:527) has `sh3_cpu_interrupt_assert()` and `sh3_cpu_interrupt_deassert()` with empty `/* TODO */` bodies. This is an exact unimplemented-callback precedent for **2, ignore silently**. [interrupt.c:67](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/core/interrupt.c:67) also defines empty `no_interrupt_assert/deassert` callbacks, although those represent intentionally absent connections.

   Conversely, [dev_cpc700.c:56](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_cpc700.c:56) updates interrupt status and propagates assertion/deassertion according to the enable mask: **7, implement**.

   **My disposition:** 2 for a narrowly scoped stub-removal round. A one-time capability diagnostic, 3, is supported by the broader reporting idiom, but the exact stub precedent is silent. Actual interrupt delivery belongs to 7 and has an additional Alpha dependency described below.

2. **BUS/cycle-type �?" decode and delegate has precedent; silently discarding bus selection also has precedent.**

   [dev_uninorth.c:64](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_uninorth.c:64) distinguishes configuration formats: one uses `bus_pci_decompose_1()`, the other decodes a primary-bus one-hot device selection, then both call `bus_pci_setaddr()`. This closely matches LCA�?Ts missing distinction.

   The shared [bus_pci_data_access()](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/bus_pci.c:109) already handles absent devices: reads return all ones at register zero and zero elsewhere; writes are dropped with an **unlatched** `fatal()` diagnostic. Thus absent devices already have a model answer, but missing LCA decoding still needs implementation.

   **Conflicts:** [dev_i80321.c:215](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_i80321.c:215) decomposes the tag and then forcibly assigns `bus = 0`. [dev_footbridge.c:476](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_footbridge.c:476) diagnoses bus 255 on every access and returns success without forwarding. Neither establishes a universal decode-and-delegate policy, and neither is �?oreport once.�??

   **My disposition:** 7 for the missing cycle decoder, followed by the existing PCI model�?Ts answer. Bare removal, 1, is insufficient: LCA�?Ts nonzero branch leaves `dev` unassigned. Also, its variable `bus` currently enumerates **`{0,1}`**, from `ioc_conf & 1`; it is the cycle selector, not the decoded secondary-bus number. [LCA decoding](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:121).

3. **RANGE/lane �?" the tree explicitly supports both accepting existing computation and declining unsupported lane operations.**

   [dev_pcc2.c:427](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pcc2.c:427) copies read bytes before dispatch. Its width guards now apply only to writes: reads retain the computed answer (**4**, achieved by removing the read refusal, **1**); wide writes call `pcc2_width_decline()` and leave state unchanged (**3**). This distinction is also recorded in [pcc2_width_probe.py](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/pcc2_width_probe.py:2).

   There is a closer lane-policy conflict within SH4: [BCR2/BCR3](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sh4.c:2305) use helpers that reject partial accesses and select the register�?Ts own bytes from wider accesses. [PCR](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sh4.c:2354) simply stores/returns the composed value without that width policy.

