# ASSESS -- `lcabadval` (HIGH): four Alpha chipset registers end the emulator process on any value outside a small accepted set, and the probe that would witness it has a confound that makes its obvious oracle lie.  Tree `GXEMUL-SEC/src/`, HEAD `83df95c` plus two uncommitted rounds touching neither this file nor its probe.  2026-09-06.

## CONTEXT AND VOCABULARY
An ordinary correctness review of an open-source machine emulator (GXemul 0.7.0, a hardened fork).  The device is the **LCA**, the core-logic
chipset of a DEC Alpha workstation; the "guest" here is firmware (SRM) or an operating system bringing the chipset up.  The defect is that the
device model calls `exit(1)` -- ending the whole emulator process -- when the guest writes a value the model does not implement.  Plain
engineering vocabulary throughout.

Tree under review: **`GXEMUL-SEC/src/`**, file `src/devices/dev_lca.c`.  Three pristine-2021 upstream copies of GXemul sit under the project
root and are NOT this fork; if your line numbers disagree with the ones below by hundreds you are reading one of those, so say so.
**Cite by grep anchor, not by line number** -- the numbers below came from an enumeration on 2026-08-31 and this file has not been re-derived
since.  `python3` in git-bash is a Windows Store stub; use `C:\Users\User00\AppData\Local\Programs\Python\Python312\python.exe`.
**VERDICT LINE FIRST.**

## READ THIS FIRST -- PLEASE DO NOT SIMPLY RATIFY
This row's own records have already been refuted once, by a seat that went and counted: it found **six** value-guarded arms where the record
said four, **eleven** live terminating calls where the record said twelve, and that **three of the six** accept exactly one value rather than
one of four -- so the ORIGINAL title was closer to right than the correction that overturned it.  Assume the numbers below may be wrong too.
**Check the premises, not just the derivations.**  Re-derive every count from the file and say what you got.

## THE DEFECT AS RECORDED (each of these is a claim to CHECK, not a given)
`dev_lca.c` carries value guards that each end in `exit(1)`, with diagnostics that say so in the author's own words -- the phrase
*"from the only implemented value"* recurs.  The enumeration on record:

| guard, as recorded | recorded site |
|---|---|
| `idata & ~1` | :206 |
| `idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN` | :226 |
| `idata != 0ULL && idata != 0x300800000ULL` | :241 |
| `idata != 0x700000ULL` | :256 |
| `idata != 0x240000000ULL` | :280 |
| `idata != 0x3ff00000ULL` | :295 |
| plus two RANGE guards that also kill: `dev >= 21` (:129) and `ofs > len` (:163) |  |

**SCOPE, stated so it is not over-claimed:** the two RANGE guards are a DIFFERENT question from the value guards and are deliberately not
analysed here.  A bounds guard that kills may be protecting a real array, in which case the disposition is decline-not-delete and deciding it
needs the array geometry.  Named, not judged.

**Why this matters and why it has escaped attention:** the single consumer is `machine_alpha.c`'s `device_add(machine, "lca")`, and there is
no Alpha rig in this tree.  *No rig means no coverage, not no bug.*  A guest here is firmware, so the reaching value is whatever that
firmware writes -- a model that implements one value and dies on the rest turns every unmodelled firmware revision into a host death.

## THE CONFOUND -- and this is the part the round turns on
`lca_interrupt_assert` and `lca_interrupt_deassert` are **bare `fatal()` + `exit(1)`**, with the whole ISA interrupt chain wired to them.  So
**any interrupt ends the host with the same exit status as the value guards under test.**  A witness that keys on the exit status therefore
cannot tell the defect from an unrelated interrupt, and every row must match the `fatal()` TEXT instead.

**THE FIRST QUESTION FOR YOU.**  A separate finding (`lcaconfwidth`) is that a 64-bit guarded value is printed through `(int)` in one of these
KILL diagnostics, so **the message can name a value that would not have killed**, and at one site names the wrong value entirely.  Given that
every row must match TEXT, **is a text-matching oracle sound at THAT arm -- or must the printf fix land FIRST, making it a prerequisite of
this round rather than a sibling of it?**

## THE SECOND QUESTION -- the accept-row count
Removing a host-fatal guard deletes the accidental tripwire that made a wrong write self-announcing, so the de-escalation clause owes an
**ACCEPT-side row per predicate the old `exit(1)` used to gate**.  An earlier census of this row dropped two predicates (`W_BASE1` and
`W_MASK1`).  **Confirm the accept-row set by re-deriving it: is it six, or four, or something else?**  Name each predicate and the accepted
value a row would drive at it.

## THE THIRD QUESTION -- the option set, all six members named before choosing
This project's rule is to name the whole option set rather than argue about two members.  For each of the six value arms:
**1 DELETE** the kill; **2 DECLINE silently**; **3 DECLINE + latched announce** (the house idiom is N=1 per device instance with a `(once)`
marker -- derive the current spelling from the tree rather than trusting any count written down); **4 ACCEPT** -- if the model already
computes the right answer and then discards it, storing and continuing is not a refusal but the correct behaviour; **5 FAULT** -- deliver the
architectural machine check, which on this chipset may be what real hardware does for an unimplemented register value, and if so it is a
genuine candidate rather than a formality; **6 BACKED ACCEPT** -- invent storage.
**Which member per arm, and why?**  Do not assume one answer fits all six.  Note there is **no Alpha architecture manual on disk**, so say
where your answer depends on documentation you do not have.

## THE FOURTH QUESTION -- the witness
A rung-3 witness has been specified but not built: target `-E alpha -e alphabook1`, since `machine_alpha.c`'s `ST_ALPHABOOK1` is the only
subtype calling `device_add(machine, "lca")`.  **Is that route real?  What does the probe have to do to reach each of the six arms, and what
controls does it need** beyond the two the ladder requires for an access witness (a liveness control that a known value returns through the
same decode, and a device-signature control that the observed value could only have come from this device)?  The confound above means it
needs at least one more: a control that distinguishes the guard's death from the interrupt path's death.

## WHAT TO ANSWER
VERDICT LINE FIRST (REAL / NOT REAL / REAL BUT DIFFERENT), then the four questions in order, then anything else.  Re-derive the counts.  If a
count in this brief is wrong, say what it is and how you got yours.  Under a page after the verdict line.

